    "answer": {
      "text": "A total solar eclipse happens because the Moon passes directly between the Earth and the Sun, aligning them in a nearly straight line. This alignment allows the Moon's shadow to fall on Earth.\n\n**Why the Moon must be near the line:**\nThe Moon orbits Earth in a plane that is tilted about 5° relative to Earth's orbit around the Sun (the ecliptic). Because of this tilt, the Moon usually passes above or below the Sun from our perspective, even when it is New. A total eclipse only occurs when the New Moon happens to be near one of the two points where the orbits cross (called nodes), allowing the Moon to block the Sun's disk completely.\n\n**Totality vs. Partial Eclipse:**\n- **Totality:** Occurs only within the narrow path of the Moon's *umbra* (the darkest part of the shadow). Here, the Sun is completely obscured.\n- **Partial Eclipse:** Occurs in the wider area covered by the Moon's *penumbra* (the lighter shadow). Here, the Sun is only partially covered. Most people see a partial eclipse; only those in the narrow path see totality.\n\n**Does every New Moon cause a total eclipse?**\nNo. Because of the orbital tilt, most New Moons occur when the Moon is too far north or south of the Sun-Earth line to block the Sun. Total eclipses are relatively rare events at any given location.\n\n**Source:**\n- [NASA Eclipse Web Site](https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEmono/geometry/) – Explains the geometry of eclipses and why they don't happen every month.",
      "sha256": "29d860d0b140c9f627fe49bacfa60c4a71eb3fe193c9a131612880889ce21de0"
    },
